Hello,

When running the latency program provided in the Xenomai source, my latency=
 numbers for 'max latency' is consistently over 30 us. I have installed Xen=
omai 2.6.0 using Linux kernel 2.6.38.8. I have attached my config for my li=
nux kernel. SMI is disabled, legacy USB support is disabled in the BIOS, an=
d the other obvious latency killers provided in the troubleshooting guide h=
ave been checked. If you need any extra information about my system setup, =
please ask. All PCI cards have been removed from the system.

When varying the period of the latency test, I can sometimes get latencies =
less than 10 us, but the the next segment will show the latency over 20 or =
30 pretty consistently.

I have set up a simple application that creates an alarm, then waits for th=
e alarm and captures the time immediately after the alarm was tripped. The =
function that the RTASK calls has the following body:

void listenerMethod(void * arg) {
  int iter=3D0;
  while(iter < NUM_RUNS) {
    rt_alarm_wait(&interruptAlarm);
    alarmTimes[iter] =3D rt_timer_read();
    ++iter;
  }
}

I have defined NUM_RUNS to be 50000, so it waits for 50000 alarms then exit=
s. The task is affined to a single CPU, but the affect I'm experiencing hap=
pens whether the task has affinity or not.

I later have a program that reads the alarmTimes and prints them to a file.=
 It just iterates through the array and writes the RTIME values as long lon=
gs using fprintf(FID, "%lld\n", alarmTimes[i]).

The RT_ALARM is created and started with the following lines:

(Defined at top of file with other constants)
RTIME FIRST_ALARM_OFFSET =3D 50000000; // Wait 50 ms before starting alarms
RTIME ALARM_INTERVAL =3D 100000; // Wait 100 us between alarms

(In main)
rt_timer_create(&interruptAlarm, "InterruptAlarm");
rt_timer_start(&interruptAlarm, FIRST_ALARM_OFFSET, ALARM_INTERVAL);

Although I haven't written it in this email, all error codes are being chec=
ked and verified to be working properly.

The file attached, latencyTimes.png, plots the diff in the latency times pe=
r sample recorded from the listenerMethod. As you can see, there are period=
ic spikes in the latency of receiving the alarm. As expected, the mean of t=
he entire dataset is 100 us because that is the period of the alarm.

I have also attached a file called latencyTimesZoomed.png. This is zoomed i=
n on one of the large latency spikes, showing that the time between higher =
latency times is exactly 1 ms because the alarm interval is 100 us and the =
spike occurs every 10 samples. The spikes that occur every 10 samples get l=
arger and larger, and they follow the curve in latencyTimes.png. The times =
less than 100 us always occur following a time greater than 100 us, and tha=
t makes perfect sense.

In the attached files, the x axis is sample number and the y axis is the ti=
me is microseconds between any 2 alarm received times (using diff in Matlab=
).

What is the problem here? This problem has been present ever since the fres=
h boot into the kernel. I believe that these periodic spikes are responsibl=
e for the /usr/xenomai/bin/latency tests max latencies being consistently a=
bove 30 us.

Again, if there is any information that I haven't provided, please ask. I h=
ave been working with this problem for several days, so I'm sure there are =
a lot of things that I've assumed you will know when there is no way that y=
ou can.

Thank you,
Troy Wolz

